 ## Overview
-Hard and Soft Depth Regularization is a training strategy for 3D Gaussian Splatting that prevents geometric detail loss and blurred appearance by constraining the spatial reshaping of Gaussian primitives rather than deforming their shapes [S1]. It sets itself apart from standard depth regularization methods (which often force Gaussian shapes to fit smooth monocular depth estimates, causing conflicts with complex color appearance) by freezing scale and rotation parameters and using two distinct rendered depth maps to independently update center positions and opacities [S1].
+Hard and Soft Depth Regularization is a geometric constraint strategy for 3D Gaussian Splatting that prevents detail loss and blurred appearance by decoupling the optimization of Gaussian centers from their opacities [S1]. It sets itself apart from standard depth regularization methods (which often deform Gaussian shapes to fit smooth monocular depth) by freezing scale and rotation parameters, thereby encouraging spatial reshaping of primitives rather than shape deformation [S1]. This approach balances coarse depth fitting with fine color appearance preservation [S1].
 
 ## How it works
